<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brandenburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brandenburg is a historical region of northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Its ruler, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>, was one of the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> who chose the emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a>, Brandenburg became the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>, which took part in the invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a> and later in the partitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brandenburg.jpg" alt="Brandenburg" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Brandenburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Brandenburg lies in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and surrounds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and the Oder forms part of its eastern border. Its main rivers are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a>. The name comes from the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a>, and its origin is unclear: it is thought to derive from the Slavic branyi, marshy, or the Germanic branda, fire. In the extinct local language Polabian the town was called Branibor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 7th century Slavic peoples are believed to have settled the area, the two principal groups being the Hevelli in the west and the Sprevane in the east. From the early 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> and his successors conquered territory up to the Oder, and in 948 Emperor Otto I established margraves over the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> there. A great uprising of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> drove the imperial forces out in 983. In 1134 Emperor Lothar III granted the Northern March to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a>, who inherited the town of Brandenburg and the lands of the Hevelli from Pribislav in 1150 and proclaimed himself ruler of the new Margraviate of Brandenburg.</p><p>The Ascanian line ended in 1320, and from 1323 to 1415 Brandenburg was under the Wittelsbachs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, under whom its margrave gained the status of prince-elector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1415 Emperor Sigismund granted the Electorate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>, which ruled until the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> its capital. Brandenburg converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in 1539, and the Hohenzollerns acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> in 1618, forming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">The Great Elector</a>, Frederick William, rebuilt and consolidated the state after the Thirty Years' War, and in 1701 his son took the title King in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> as Frederick I. Brandenburg remained the core of the Kingdom of Prussia and, from 1815 to 1947, was a province of Prussia. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line">Oder–Neisse line</a> were ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. East Germany dissolved the state in 1952, and it was re-established in the Federal Republic on 3 October 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Brandenburg is a landlocked state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> as its capital and about 2.5 million residents. Other major towns are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottbus">Cottbus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel">Brandenburg an der Havel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> (Oder). In the southeast, part of the historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a> has two official languages, German and Lower Sorbian. A proposal to unify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and Brandenburg was rejected in a referendum in 1996, and the two states still cooperate on many matters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brandenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Conrad and Frederick at first held the inheritance together and then divided it, probably by about 1218: Conrad took the burgraviate of Nuremberg and founded the Franconian line, which later ruled <b>Brandenburg</b>, Prussia and the German Empire, while Frederick kept the ancestral Swabian lands as Count of Zollern.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The most persistent threat came from the margraves of <b>Brandenburg</b>, who in 1231 obtained from Emperor Frederick II a claim to overlordship and took territory such as the Uckermark by the Treaty of Landin in 1250.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He also campaigned against the Slavs east of the Elbe, taking <b>Brandenburg</b> in the winter of 928–929.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg lived from trade and from the Elbe, which connected it to <b>Brandenburg</b>, Saxony and Bohemia upstream and to the North Sea downstream.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Prussia was created from the lands that the Hohenzollern electors of <b>Brandenburg</b> had gathered over the previous century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Brandenburg</b> had an African colony: from 1683 its fort of Gross Friedrichsburg stood on the Gold Coast in today’s Ghana, until the Prussian king sold it to the Dutch in 1717.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
